  "summary": "Multiple issues in command quoting on Windows",
  "details": "ANT-2026-R7FKP7RG Win32 quote_cmd_filename misses pipe/redirect metacharacters\n\nANT-2026-SRZZ62SD Windows batch-file argument injection via maybe_quote/make_cmdline\n\nANT-2026-JPEWMPAB Win32 argument quoting leaves embedded CR/LF unescaped\n\nAlso includes the fix for [OSEC-2026-05](https://osv.dev/vulnerability/OSEC-2026-05)\n\n`Filename.quote_command` returns a quoted command line, suitable for use as an argument to `Sys.command`, `Unix.system`, and the `Unix.open_process` functions.\nHowever on Windows certain characters weren't properly escaped.\n\n`Unix.create_process` can invoke `.bat` or `.cmd` programs on Windows, and certain characters were accepted that cannot be quoted safely.\n\n## Fix\n\n`Filename.quote_command` has been updated to correctly quote arguments.\n`Unix.create_process*` has been updated to reject arguments that cannot be safely quoted by raising `Failure`:\n>  if the program [prog] is a [.bat] or [.cmd]\n>  script and either [prog] or [args] contain characters that cannot be\n>  securely passed to a script, such as [\" | & , ; < = > ^ % \"].\n>  If you really need to pass these characters to a script, consider\n>  using {!Unix.system} instead.\n\n## Am I affected?\n\nIf you use `Filename.quote_command` on Windows with user supplied arguments.\nIf you use `Unix.create_process*` or `Unix.open_process*` on Windows with `.bat` or `.cmd` programs, and user supplied arguments.\n\n## Timeline\n\n- 2026-08-24: vulnerability was found\n- 2026-08-27: forwarded report to security@ocaml.org\n- 2029-09-05: fix merged to trunk",
